### 5.3 Budgeting system

I would make this one of the strongest parts of the application.

Do not force everyone to use one budgeting methodology. People have different income patterns, financial responsibilities and levels of budgeting experience.

Instead, provide a few clear budgeting methods that use the same transaction records.

#### Budget method A: Category budgeting

This is the traditional approach.

The user creates spending categories and sets a limit for each one.

For example:

| Category      | Monthly limit |
| ------------- | ------------- |
| Rent          | KES 7,000     |
| Food          | KES 8,000     |
| Transport     | KES 4,000     |
| Utilities     | KES 2,000     |
| Internet      | KES 3,000     |
| Entertainment | KES 2,000     |
| Miscellaneous | KES 2,000     |

The app tracks spending against each limit.

A category should display:

- Allocated budget.
- Total spent.
- Amount remaining.
- Percentage used.
- Expected end-of-period spending.

The progress indicator should communicate risk clearly.

For example, users might choose to receive an in-app warning when a category reaches 80% of its allocation. This threshold must be configurable.

The system must not imply that spending 80% is automatically bad. A rent category may legitimately be 100% used early in the month.

The application should distinguish between categories where spending is expected to be completed early and categories where overspending is a concern.

#### Budget method B: Flexible budgeting

Some users do not want to manage 20 or 30 individual spending limits.

A flexible budget allows them to group spending into broad categories.

For example:

- Fixed commitments.
- Non-monthly expenses.
- Flexible spending.
- Savings and investments.

The application then shows how much flexible spending remains after fixed obligations and planned saving.

This is useful for users who want more freedom and less administration.

Monarch's Flex Budgeting is a useful reference for this approach.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://help.monarch.com\&sz=32)

Monarch Money+1



#### Budget method C: Zero-based budgeting

This method asks users to give all planned income a purpose.

The application calculates the amount of income that remains unallocated.

For example:

| Allocation         | Amount     |
| ------------------ | ---------- |
| Income             | KES 50,000 |
| Rent and bills     | KES 15,000 |
| Food and transport | KES 12,000 |
| Debt repayments    | KES 5,000  |
| Savings            | KES 8,000  |
| Investments        | KES 5,000  |
| Flexible spending  | KES 5,000  |
| Unallocated        | KES 0      |

The point is not that the user must spend every shilling. Savings, debt repayment and investment contributions are legitimate uses of money.

The app should make it easy to see whether the user has given all their expected income a purpose.

This is one of the most useful ideas from YNAB.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.ynab.com\&sz=32)

YNAB+1



#### Budget method D: Pay-cycle budgeting

Many people do not receive income neatly at the beginning of each calendar month.

The app should allow users to budget around their actual paydays.

Supported cycles should include:

- Weekly.
- Fortnightly.
- Twice a month.
- Monthly.
- Irregular income.
- User-defined periods.

A person who gets paid on the 25th should not be forced to think of the 1st as the beginning of their financial month.

This is particularly important for users whose income arrives on different dates.

#### Budget rollover

Users should decide what happens to unused money at the end of a budgeting period.

For example:

A user allocates KES 5,000 for entertainment but spends only KES 3,000.

They can choose to:

- Carry the remaining KES 2,000 into the next period.
- Return it to their available budget.
- Reallocate it to a savings goal.

The same principle should apply to overspending. Users should be able to see how a budget shortfall affects the following period.

Do not automatically hide previous overspending when a new month starts.

#### Budget templates

The application should include editable templates for common budgeting approaches:

- Basic monthly budget.
- Student budget.
- Salary-based budget.
- Irregular-income budget.
- Family household budget structure, without enabling shared accounts.
- Freelancer budget.
- Small-business owner personal budget.
- Zero-based budget.

A template should simply create a starting category structure and suggested allocations. It must not assume that its suggested amounts are correct for the user.

#### Budget forecasting

This is where budgeting becomes more useful than simple expense tracking.

The application should estimate the user's likely end-of-period spending using:

- Transactions already recorded.
- Scheduled payments entered by the user.
- Historical spending patterns.
- Remaining days in the period.
- The user's current budget allocations.

For example:

A user has spent KES 6,000 on food by the 10th of the month. Their food budget is KES 12,000.

The application can estimate that the user might spend approximately KES 18,000 by the end of the month if their current rate continues.

It should show that this is an estimate, explain what it is based on, and allow the user to adjust it.

Wallet's documented predicted-expense feature is a useful reference for this capability.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://support.budgetbakers.com\&sz=32)

Wallet Help Center+1



### 5.4 Recurring payments and financial calendar

Users should not have to remember every regular obligation manually.

Allow them to create recurring entries.

Each recurring entry should contain:

- Payment name.
- Amount or estimated amount.
- Category.
- Account.
- Frequency.
- Due date.
- Start date.
- Optional end date.
- Whether the amount is fixed or variable.
- Whether it is still active.

Examples include rent, electricity, internet, subscriptions, insurance and monthly loan repayments.

The calendar should have two important functions.

First: upcoming obligations.

Users can see what payments are expected during the week or month.

Second: the actual payment record.

When the user pays an obligation, they should be able to mark it as paid and create or link the actual transaction.

The app must avoid recording the same payment twice.

For variable bills, the user should be able to enter an estimated value, then replace it with the actual value when paid.

Also include a monthly recurring-payment summary. This should show how much money is committed to recurring obligations and how much remains for flexible spending.

### 5.5 Savings goals

Savings should have its own module instead of being treated as just another expense category.

A user should be able to create a goal by entering:

- Goal name.
- Target amount.
- Current amount saved.
- Target date.
- Currency.
- Priority.
- Associated savings account, if relevant.
- Optional notes or description.

The application should calculate:

- Percentage complete.
- Amount remaining.
- Required saving per week or month.
- Estimated completion date at the current saving rate.
- Whether the goal is on schedule.

For example, a user wants to save KES 120,000 for a car deposit within 12 months.

The app should calculate that the user needs to save KES 10,000 per month, assuming no existing savings and no investment return.

It should also let the user change the target date or amount and immediately recalculate the required contribution.

#### Multiple goals

Users should be able to manage several goals simultaneously.

Examples:

- Emergency fund.
- Rent deposit.
- Education.
- Vehicle purchase.
- Holiday.
- Business capital.
- Home deposit.
- Medical reserve.

The user should be able to prioritise them.

#### Emergency fund

Include a dedicated emergency fund view.

Allow a user to set a target based on a number of months of essential expenses, rather than only entering an arbitrary target.

For instance, the user might choose three, six or nine months of essential expenses.

The app calculates the target using their recorded essential spending. Users can adjust the result.

### 5.6 Debt management

Debt must be treated as a complete financial module.

It should not just be a number displayed on a dashboard.

Users should be able to add different forms of debt:

- Personal loans.
- Bank loans.
- Student loans.
- HELB.
- Credit cards.
- Car loans.
- Mortgages.
- Mobile loans.
- Loans from friends or family.
- Business-related personal guarantees or obligations, where the user chooses to track them.

Each debt record should include:

| Field               | Function                  |
| ------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Debt name           | Identifies the obligation |
| Original amount     | Initial amount borrowed   |
| Outstanding balance | Current unpaid balance    |
| Interest rate       | Annual or specified rate  |
| Repayment amount    | Planned regular payment   |
| Frequency           | Weekly, monthly or custom |
| Due date            | Next repayment date       |
| Loan start date     | When the debt began       |
| Expected end date   | Estimated payoff date     |
| Lender              | Optional lender details   |
| Notes               | Other loan information    |

#### Debt payoff simulator

The app should allow users to test how different repayment amounts affect their debt.

For example:

- Current monthly payment: KES 3,000.
- Proposed payment: KES 5,000.
- Additional payment: KES 2,000.

The simulator should estimate:

- New payoff date.
- Interest saved.
- Number of payments remaining.
- Total expected repayment.
- Difference compared with the existing plan.

This should be calculated using the loan's entered interest and repayment terms, with assumptions shown clearly.

#### Debt repayment strategies

Include two recognised methods.

Debt avalanche: prioritise the debt with the highest interest rate. This can reduce interest costs, assuming other minimum payments are maintained.

Debt snowball: prioritise the smallest outstanding balance. This can give users quicker visible progress.

The application should compare the methods where it has sufficient loan information.

YNAB's debt planner demonstrates the value of letting users explore extra-payment scenarios instead of merely displaying a loan balance.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.ynab.com\&sz=32)

YNAB+1



#### Debt-payment accounting

A repayment should be divided into principal and interest whenever that information is available.

The principal reduces the outstanding liability. The interest is an expense.

This distinction is essential for correct financial reporting.

### 5.7 Spending insights

The Insights area should help users understand why their financial position is changing.

It should answer more than "you spent KES 4,000 on food".

Recommended insights include:

Spending by category: where the money goes.

Spending by merchant: which businesses receive the most money.

Spending by day: which days the user typically spends more.

Spending by week: how spending changes during a month.

Spending by month: long-term changes in spending behaviour.

Needs versus wants: allow transactions to be labelled, with the category breakdown available for comparison.

Fixed versus flexible expenses: help users understand how much of their income is committed.

Income versus expenses: show whether the user consistently spends less than they earn.

Recurring expenses: show how much of the user's income is committed before discretionary spending begins.

The app should allow the user to tap a chart or statistic and inspect the transactions behind it.

This is an important usability pattern in modern reporting products such as Monarch.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://help.monarch.com\&sz=32)

Monarch Money+1



## 6. Investment Management environment

This environment must feel more like a dedicated investment portfolio manager.

It should be useful to a beginner holding a single money market fund, as well as an experienced investor who owns stocks, bonds, ETFs, real estate and other assets.

The design should not assume that every user is an active trader.

The primary goal is to help people understand their investments, their returns and their wealth.

I recommend the following navigation:

| Area      | Main purpose                      |
| --------- | --------------------------------- |
| Portfolio | Total investment position         |
| Holdings  | Individual assets and investments |
| Activity  | Investment transactions           |
| Income    | Dividends and interest            |
| Analysis  | Returns, allocation and risk      |
| Watchlist | Assets the user wants to monitor  |

### 6.1 Investment dashboard

The dashboard should immediately communicate the condition of the user's portfolio.

It should show:

Total portfolio value

The current recorded or estimated value of all investments included in the portfolio.

Total invested capital

How much the user has contributed, adjusted for withdrawals and the specific return method used.

Total gain or loss

The difference between the portfolio's value and its relevant invested capital, with dividends, interest, fees and withdrawals treated correctly.

Total investment income

The dividends, interest and distributions received over a selected period.

Portfolio allocation

The percentage of the portfolio held in each asset class.

Performance history

A graph showing how portfolio value and returns have changed over time.

Top holdings

The assets making up the largest share of the portfolio.

Upcoming income

Expected dividends, coupon payments or other investment distributions where the user has entered the relevant information.

Valuation freshness

Show when a price or balance was last updated. An old manual valuation must not appear to be a live market price.

### 6.2 Portfolio and account organisation

Users should be able to create multiple investment portfolios.

For example:

- Long-term wealth portfolio.
- Dividend portfolio.
- Retirement portfolio.
- Speculative investment portfolio.
- Global stock portfolio.
- Kenyan investment portfolio.

Within each portfolio, users should be able to create or assign investment accounts.

Examples:

- NSE brokerage account.
- International brokerage account.
- Money market fund account.
- Treasury investment account.
- SACCO membership.
- Crypto wallet.
- Private investment account.

This organisation should make it possible to review the overall portfolio or focus on one investment strategy.

The user should be able to see the same holding in the overall consolidated portfolio without duplicating it across accounts.

### 6.3 Supported investment and asset classes

This is where I recommend going beyond ordinary stock portfolio trackers.

The user should be able to track assets that form part of their broader wealth, not just assets with a stock ticker.

| Asset class          | Information to track                                                             |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| NSE shares           | Company, ticker, quantity, purchase price, fees, dividends and current valuation |
| International shares | Company, exchange, ticker, quantity, purchase price and currency                 |
| ETFs                 | Fund, ticker, units, purchase cost, market value and distributions               |
| Money market funds   | Provider, invested amount, units or balance, returns, fees and valuation date    |
| Treasury bills       | Investment amount, discount or yield, maturity date and redemption amount        |
| Government bonds     | Principal, coupon rate, payment schedule, maturity and market or estimated value |
| SACCO shares         | Member shares, deposits, dividends, interest and updated member balance          |
| REITs                | Units, purchase cost, valuation, distributions and currency                      |
| Cryptocurrency       | Asset, quantity, cost basis, valuation and exchange or wallet                    |
| Real estate          | Property value, ownership percentage, rental income and related debt             |
| Private businesses   | Ownership percentage, invested capital, estimated valuation and distributions    |
| Private funds        | Contributions, commitments, capital calls, distributions and estimated value     |
| Precious metals      | Metal type, quantity, purchase price and current estimated price                 |
| Other assets         | Description, purchase cost, estimated value, ownership and valuation date        |

This should be an extensible asset model. Do not hard-code the entire application around public stocks and cryptocurrencies.

#### Important distinction: investment assets versus personal assets

The application should distinguish between assets held primarily to grow wealth and assets owned for personal use.

For example:

- A rental property may be a wealth-producing investment.
- A home occupied by its owner is an asset, but its value is not necessarily readily available for investment.
- A vehicle is part of net worth, but it should not automatically be considered an investment.
- Jewellery or personal valuables may be part of the balance sheet without being productive investments.

This distinction becomes very important when calculating investable net worth and financial freedom.

Kubera's approach to tracking the complete balance sheet, while distinguishing investable assets from total net worth, is a particularly useful reference.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.kubera.com\&sz=32)

Kubera+1



### 6.4 Manual investment entry

The first release should not depend on brokerage integrations.

The user should be able to add an investment manually.

For a share purchase, for example, the user enters:

- Company or ticker.
- Exchange.
- Quantity purchased.
- Purchase date.
- Purchase price per share.
- Transaction fees.
- Currency.
- Investment account.
- Optional notes.

The application records the transaction and updates the holding.

When the user buys another batch of the same stock at a different price, the application should add to the existing holding while preserving the individual purchase records.

The same principle should apply to other investment classes, using fields appropriate to each asset.

Users must be able to record investments that do not have market prices. For these assets, the application should support manual valuations and keep a history of valuation changes.

### 6.5 Investment activity and transaction types

The investment ledger should support more than purchases and sales.

At minimum:

- Buy.
- Sell.
- Deposit or contribution.
- Withdrawal.
- Dividend received.
- Interest received.
- Distribution received.
- Investment fees.
- Tax withheld.
- Stock split.
- Bonus shares.
- Rights issue.
- Capital call.
- Maturity or redemption.
- Manual valuation adjustment.

Every transaction should be connected to the relevant investment account and asset where appropriate.

For example, a user buys shares for KES 10,000 and pays KES 100 in transaction fees.

The app should record the share acquisition and the cost incurred. It should not simply treat the full KES 10,100 as an ordinary living expense in Money Management.

The app's shared financial foundation should understand that this is a movement of money into an investment, not consumption.

### 6.6 Portfolio performance

This is a feature that deserves special attention.

Many simple investment trackers show only the difference between the original purchase price and the current price.

That is insufficient for a serious long-term portfolio manager.

The application should provide different performance views.

Unrealised gain or loss

The gain or loss on investments the user still holds.

Realised gain or loss

The gain or loss from investments that have been sold or redeemed.

Dividend and interest income

The cash income generated by the holdings.

Fees and taxes

The costs associated with owning, buying and selling investments.

Currency impact

The effect of foreign exchange movements on investments held in other currencies.

Total return

The overall return, taking account of the relevant cash flows, investment income and valuation changes.

The interface should distinguish simple gain/loss from investment return calculations so users do not confuse one with another.

As the product matures, support more advanced performance methodologies:

- Time-weighted return, useful for evaluating investment performance while reducing the distortion caused by the timing of deposits and withdrawals.
- Money-weighted return or internal rate of return, useful for understanding the user's actual investment experience, including when money was added and withdrawn.

For a beginner, the app should show a straightforward return summary. Advanced calculation methods should be available in the detailed analysis area.

Sharesight and Ghostfolio demonstrate the importance of dividend-inclusive and transaction-aware performance analysis.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://www.sharesight.com\&sz=32)

Sharesight+1



### 6.7 Dividends and investment income

This should be a dedicated feature, not a small item hidden under individual holdings.

Users who invest for income need to understand how much their portfolio generates.

The module should include:

- Dividends received.
- Interest received.
- Distributions.
- Gross amount.
- Withholding tax, if known.
- Net amount received.
- Payment date.
- Related holding.
- Currency.
- Income frequency.
- Historical income.

The user should be able to see monthly, quarterly and annual investment income.

For example, a person may own several dividend-paying stocks and want to know how much income they have generated over the past twelve months.

The app should calculate this from their recorded receipts.

It should also support projected income where sufficient dividend or interest information has been manually entered.

Projected income must be clearly labelled as an estimate, not a guaranteed payment.

### 6.8 Investment income calendar

Build a calendar dedicated to investment income and relevant asset dates.

It should display:

- Expected dividend dates.
- Recorded dividend payments.
- Coupon payment dates.
- Treasury bill maturity dates.
- Bond maturity dates.
- Fund distribution dates.
- Other investment events entered by the user.

The calendar should let users distinguish between expected, confirmed and received income.

This is especially useful for dividend investors and people with fixed-income portfolios.

Delta documents a financial calendar, while Sharesight has dividend and future-income reporting features. These are valuable patterns to bring together.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://delta.app\&sz=32)

Delta by etoro+1



### 6.9 Asset allocation

Users should be able to see exactly how their investment portfolio is distributed.

For example:

| Asset class        | Value       | Allocation |
| ------------------ | ----------- | ---------- |
| Stocks             | KES 200,000 | 40%        |
| Money market funds | KES 100,000 | 20%        |
| Government bonds   | KES 100,000 | 20%        |
| ETFs               | KES 50,000  | 10%        |
| Crypto             | KES 25,000  | 5%         |
| Other investments  | KES 25,000  | 5%         |
| Total              | KES 500,000 | 100%       |

The app should allow users to view allocation by:

- Asset class.
- Individual holding.
- Country or market.
- Currency.
- Sector.
- Investment account.
- Portfolio strategy.

This would help a person recognise overconcentration.

For example, someone may own ten different stocks, but all ten may belong to the same sector. Merely counting holdings would give a misleading impression of diversification.

### 6.10 Portfolio risk and concentration analysis

This should be presented as an educational analytical tool, not as a promise of investment safety.

Potential indicators include:

- Largest holding as a percentage of the portfolio.
- Top five holdings as a percentage of the portfolio.
- Asset-class concentration.
- Country exposure.
- Currency exposure.
- Sector exposure.
- High-volatility asset allocation, where reliable classification data exists.
- Liquidity classification.
- Percentage of assets with old or estimated valuations.

The app should explain why each indicator matters.

For example:

"Your three largest holdings represent 68% of your portfolio."

That is much more informative than displaying a mysterious risk score.

### 6.11 Watchlist

Users should be able to monitor investments they do not yet own.

A watchlist item should include:

- Asset name.
- Ticker or identifier, where available.
- Market or provider.
- Current or last-known market price, if available.
- User's preferred entry price.
- Reason for monitoring.
- Notes.

Watchlist assets must be separate from actual holdings.

Adding a company to a watchlist must not change portfolio value or investment performance.

For the first release, a basic manual watchlist could work. Automated price updates and more advanced monitoring can be added after the main tracking system is reliable.

### 6.12 Investment research and education

A useful later addition is an educational section explaining basic investment concepts.

Examples:

- What is a dividend?
- What is dividend yield?
- What is compound growth?
- What is a money market fund?
- What is a Treasury bill?
- What does diversification mean?
- What is the difference between realised and unrealised gains?
- Why can an investment's value fall?

This should be an educational library, not a feed encouraging users to constantly buy and sell.

The product should avoid pretending that a simple algorithm knows which investment a person should purchase.

## 7. The shared wealth engine

Although the user must experience two separate environments, the application needs one unified financial calculation system underneath them.

I would make this the most carefully designed part of the product.

### 7.1 Unified net worth

Net worth should be calculated as:

\\[ \text{Net Worth} = \text{Total Assets} - \text{Total Liabilities} \\]

Assets can include:

- Cash and bank balances.
- Savings.
- Investments.
- Real estate.
- SACCO balances.
- Business ownership.
- Other personal assets.

Liabilities can include:

- Personal loans.
- Student loans.
- Mortgages.
- Credit cards.
- Car loans.
- Other outstanding obligations.

The app should present a clear breakdown of these amounts.

### 7.2 Total net worth versus investable net worth

These must be separate figures.

Total net worth includes the user's assets minus their debts.

Investable net worth should focus on assets that are reasonably available to be invested or converted into investable capital, less relevant liabilities, according to a clearly documented definition.

For example, a person's house may add KES 10 million to their net worth, but that does not mean they have KES 10 million available to invest.

The app should not allow personal property to inflate investment capacity.

### 7.3 Financial timeline

This should be a chronological record of meaningful financial milestones.

Examples:

- First salary.
- First recorded investment.
- First KES 100,000 saved.
- First KES 1 million net worth.
- First debt paid off.
- First dividend received.
- First positive monthly cash flow.
- First year with positive net worth growth.

Users should be able to view these milestones over time.

This is not merely a motivational feature. It gives the user a historical record of how their financial position developed.

### 7.4 Financial health overview

I recommend a transparent financial health dashboard rather than an opaque single score.

It should evaluate areas such as:

- Savings rate.
- Emergency fund coverage.
- Debt-to-income ratio.
- Expense-to-income ratio.
- Budget consistency.
- Debt repayment progress.
- Investment contribution consistency.
- Asset diversification.
- Net worth direction.

Each indicator should have a clear explanation and a visible calculation.

For example:

Emergency fund coverage: 1.8 months

This means the user's recorded emergency savings cover approximately 1.8 months of their essential expenses, using the current recorded spending pattern.

This is actionable. A generic score of 72/100 is not.

Where the app does provide an overall Financial Health Score, users must be able to see the underlying factors, weights and missing information. A lack of data should never be interpreted as a financial failure.

### 7.5 Wealth velocity

This could become one of the product's most distinctive features.

Wealth velocity should measure how quickly the user's net worth is changing over time.

For example, show:

- Change in net worth over the last month.
- Change over the last three months.
- Change over the last twelve months.
- Average monthly net worth increase.
- Contribution from savings and investments.
- Contribution from market or valuation changes.
- Change in liabilities.

It is important to separate money the user added from money their investments generated.

If net worth grows by KES 50,000, users should be able to understand how much came from additional savings, investment gains, debt reduction and asset valuation changes.

### 7.6 Financial freedom tracker

This should help users explore their long-term financial independence goals.

The user should be able to enter:

- Desired monthly income in retirement or financial independence.
- Current investment assets.
- Expected regular investment contributions.
- Desired target date.
- Assumed investment return.
- Inflation assumption.
- Expected withdrawal rate, if they choose to use one.

The app can estimate the capital required and the user's potential progress towards that target.

For example, it could show the estimated portfolio needed to generate a particular monthly income.

However, it must clearly identify assumptions. Inflation, taxes, market returns, investment income and withdrawal rates are uncertain. The application must not present a projection as a guaranteed future result.

### 7.7 What-if financial simulator

This could be another major differentiator.

Users should be able to explore hypothetical decisions before changing their real financial records.

Examples:

- What happens if I increase my monthly savings by KES 2,000?
- What happens if I reduce food spending by KES 1,500?
- How much earlier could I pay off my debt if I make additional payments?
- What if I invest KES 5,000 per month for ten years?
- What if I increase my monthly investment contribution by 10% every year?
- What if my income falls by 20%?
- Can I afford to move into a more expensive house?
- What happens if I buy a car using a loan?

The simulator should produce different scenarios and explain the assumptions used.

It must not modify the user's real transactions, budgets, balances or investment holdings.

### 7.8 Cross-environment financial movement

This is where the two environments become genuinely powerful together.

Consider this sequence:

1. User receives KES 50,000 in Money Management.
2. User budgets KES 8,000 for savings.
3. User sets aside KES 5,000 for investments.
4. User records the transfer of KES 5,000 into an investment account.
5. The investment environment recognises a KES 5,000 contribution.
6. The net worth dashboard reflects the movement from cash to investments.

The important thing is that this contribution does not create fictitious income or an additional expense.

The user should be able to see how everyday money decisions support longer-term wealth accumulation.

## 8. The global voice assistant

The voice assistant should be a defining feature of the product, not just a microphone icon that converts speech into a search query.

The idea is to let users interact with their finances naturally, without having to navigate through multiple screens for routine actions.

It should work across both environments and understand the context of the current screen.

For now, I will call it the Assistant. Its final name and wake phrase should be decided as part of branding.

### 8.1 How the user activates it

There should be three possible ways to activate the assistant.

A. Wake word

The user says a chosen phrase, such as "Hey [app name]".

The app recognises the phrase and activates the assistant.

B. Push-to-talk

The user taps a microphone button and speaks.

This is essential even if wake-word support is implemented. It provides a predictable alternative when wake-word detection is unavailable or disabled.

C. Assistant button

The assistant should have a persistent, easily accessible control in both environments.

Tapping it opens the assistant interaction panel, where the user can speak or type a command.

The assistant must not be restricted to a single dashboard or environment.

### 8.2 What the assistant should understand

It should understand commands in several groups.

#### Group 1: Navigation

The assistant should be able to move the user around the application.

Examples:

- "Open my investments."
- "Take me to my budget."
- "Show my debts."
- "Open my dividend income."
- "Go to my savings goals."
- "Show my net worth."

Switching environments through voice should use the same transition as the pull-down gesture.

#### Group 2: Financial questions

The assistant should answer questions using the user's recorded financial information.

Examples:

- "How much did I spend this month?"
- "How much do I have left in my food budget?"
- "How much do I owe?"
- "What is my current net worth?"
- "How much have I invested?"
- "How much dividend income did I receive this year?"
- "Which category takes the largest share of my income?"
- "How much can I safely spend before payday?"
- "How much do I need to save every month to reach my goal?"

The answer should be based on recorded data, not invented figures.

Where it is useful, the assistant should explain its calculation.

For example:

User: "How much can I spend this week?"

Assistant: "You have KES 6,500 remaining in your flexible spending allowance for this week. KES 2,000 is already planned for upcoming expenses, leaving KES 4,500 uncommitted."

The assistant should show which categories and obligations were included.

#### Group 3: Recording expenses

The assistant should be able to create a transaction from speech.

Example:

User: "Record 450 shillings for lunch."

The assistant should identify:

- Type: Expense.
- Amount: KES 450.
- Category: Food.
- Date: Today.
- Account: The default selected account, or a request for the account if none is appropriate.

It should then display a compact confirmation preview.

Assistant: "I am recording a KES 450 food expense from your M-PESA account. Should I save it?"

The user confirms, and the transaction is recorded.

This matters because voice recognition can misunderstand numbers, names and categories. A transaction should not silently appear in the financial ledger just because the assistant thinks it heard something correctly.

#### Group 4: Recording income

Examples:

- "Record my salary of 45,000 shillings."
- "Add 2,500 shillings from freelance work."
- "Record a dividend of 1,200 shillings."
- "Add 5,000 to my business income."

The assistant should route each entry to the correct environment and transaction type.

#### Group 5: Budget management

Examples:

- "Create a food budget of 8,000 shillings."
- "Increase my transport budget to 5,000."
- "How much have I used in my entertainment budget?"
- "Set a savings goal of 100,000 shillings."
- "How much should I save weekly to reach my goal?"

Any action that changes an existing budget should be previewed before being saved.

#### Group 6: Investment management

Examples:

- "How much are my investments worth?"
- "Record a purchase of 20 shares."
- "How much dividend income have I earned?"
- "Show me my largest investments."
- "What percentage of my portfolio is in stocks?"
- "Record an interest payment of 800 shillings."
- "When does my Treasury bill mature?"

Investment entries must be recorded as investment activity, not ordinary spending.

#### Group 7: Financial scenarios

The assistant should eventually answer questions such as:

- "What happens if I invest 5,000 shillings every month?"
- "How much could my portfolio be worth in ten years?"
- "What if I pay an extra 2,000 on my loan?"
- "How much do I need to save to buy a car next year?"
- "What if my income increases by 10%?"

These should use the app's financial simulator, not generate unsupported financial projections.

### 8.3 The assistant must understand context

The assistant should understand which environment the user is currently in.

For example, when the user is in Money Management and says:

"How much have I invested?"

The assistant should understand that this question requires information from Investment Management.

It should answer directly or transition to the relevant portfolio screen, depending on the nature of the request.

Similarly, if a user is reviewing a particular budget and asks:

"How much did I spend here last month?"

The assistant should understand that "here" refers to the currently selected budget category.

This is contextual interaction, rather than merely processing isolated voice commands.

### 8.4 Confirmation and permission rules

The assistant needs clear boundaries.

| Action                                        | Required behaviour                    |
| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Open a screen                                 | Execute immediately                   |
| Show a financial statistic                    | Answer immediately                    |
| Explain a report                              | Answer immediately                    |
| Create an expense                             | Preview and confirm                   |
| Create an income record                       | Preview and confirm                   |
| Change a budget                               | Preview and confirm                   |
| Record an investment transaction              | Preview and confirm                   |
| Change an asset valuation                     | Preview and confirm                   |
| Delete financial records                      | Require explicit confirmation         |
| Delete an entire account or financial history | Strong confirmation and clear warning |

The assistant should never execute a financial action that it has not correctly identified.

It should not transfer funds, make payments, buy investments or sell investments. The application is a financial management and tracking system, not a payment or trading platform.

### 8.5 Privacy requirements for the voice assistant

This part is extremely important because the application deals with sensitive financial information.

I recommend the following:

- The microphone should have a clearly visible active state.
- The user must be able to turn off wake-word listening.
- The user should be able to use push-to-talk instead.
- Raw audio should not be stored by default.
- Personal financial records should not be sent to an external AI service without explicit disclosure and consent.
- Commands involving money or records should be processed with predictable validation and confirmation.
- The user should be able to see what action the assistant is about to perform.
- The application should never listen secretly.

Where possible, wake-word recognition and basic command processing should happen on the device. More complex conversational analysis can be considered separately, with an explicit privacy decision.

### 8.6 A technical reality about background wake words

There is one significant implementation constraint that should be planned for now.

A mobile application cannot be assumed to have unrestricted access to the microphone while it is in the background.

Android restricts starting microphone foreground services from the background, and newer versions impose further checks on microphone access. Apple's iOS background execution is also limited to permitted execution modes; it does not provide a general-purpose guarantee that any third-party app can continuously listen for its own wake phrase.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://developer.android.com\&sz=32)

Android Developers+2



Therefore, I recommend a staged approach:

Initial release

- Push-to-talk.
- Assistant button.
- Wake-word activation while the app is active, where supported.
- On-device command recognition where practical.

Later enhancement

- Investigate background wake-word detection separately for Android and iOS.
- Make it opt-in.
- Test battery use, microphone indicators, platform restrictions and behaviour across device manufacturers.
- Provide a reliable fallback whenever background recognition is unavailable.

We should not make a product promise that "Hey [app name]" will work continuously from the locked screen on every Android and iPhone. That cannot be guaranteed by the normal mobile app architecture.

## 9. Mobile experience and visual design

We should design this product for the phone from the beginning.

It should not be a desktop-style financial dashboard compressed into a narrow screen.

### 9.1 Separate visual identities

The two environments should have recognisably different identities.

For example:

| Design area          | Money Management                    | Investment Management                   |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| Primary mood         | Calm, clear and practical           | Analytical, composed and precise        |
| Main emphasis        | Spending and available money        | Portfolio and performance               |
| Main interactions    | Quick entry and budget review       | Holding review and investment analysis  |
| Main visual elements | Budget progress, balances and bills | Allocation charts, holdings and returns |
| Navigation           | Money-related actions               | Investment-related tools                |

The colours should distinguish the environments without making them look like unrelated products.

I would choose restrained, professional colours rather than intense neon colours, excessive gradients or stereotypical AI interface styling.

### 9.2 Mobile navigation

Each environment should have its own navigation structure.

The Money Management navigation should make the most common activities immediately accessible: viewing the dashboard, recording activity, checking budgets and reviewing financial insights.

The Investment Management navigation should prioritise the portfolio, holdings, investment activity, income and analysis.

The global Assistant and environment switcher should remain accessible without taking too much screen space.

The main navigation must not become overcrowded with every available module.

### 9.3 Quick actions

Quick actions should reduce the number of steps needed for common tasks.

In Money Management, these might include:

- Add expense.
- Add income.
- Transfer money.
- View budget.
- Add savings.

In Investment Management:

- Add investment.
- Record purchase.
- Record sale.
- Record dividend.
- Update valuation.

Do not hide important actions behind multiple menus.

### 9.4 Haptic feedback

Use subtle haptic feedback for meaningful interactions:

- Environment switching.
- Successful transaction confirmation.
- Completing a goal.
- Reaching a defined budget threshold.
- Saving an investment record.

Avoid continuous vibration or excessive feedback.

### 9.5 Dark and light themes

The application should support:

- Light mode.
- Dark mode.
- Follow system preference.

Both environments should be fully designed in both themes.

Dark mode should not simply be a black background with bright coloured text. Text, borders, charts, secondary values and transaction indicators should remain easy to distinguish.

### 9.6 Accessibility

The app must not rely solely on colour or gestures.

Include:

- Screen-reader labels.
- Sufficient text contrast.
- Adjustable text scaling.
- Clear touch targets.
- Reduced-motion support.
- Text labels for financial chart indicators.
- An accessible alternative to the environment-switch gesture.
- Clear distinction between positive, negative and neutral values.

For example, investment losses should not be represented by red alone. The interface should also show a minus sign and descriptive text.

### 9.7 Mobile performance

The application should feel responsive even when users have years of transaction records.

Important performance requirements include:

- Fast opening.
- Smooth scrolling.
- Responsive charts.
- No unnecessary full-screen refreshes.
- Efficient transaction filtering.
- Quick switching between environments.
- Stable offline operation.
- No long waiting periods for simple calculations.

A user should not have to wait for an external AI service to calculate how much they spent in a category. Basic financial calculations should happen within the app's financial engine.

## 10. Kenya-first, globally ready

The application should launch with Kenya as its initial market, but it must not be designed so narrowly that international expansion requires rebuilding core financial functions.

### 10.1 Kenya-first defaults

On first setup, the app should support:

- Country: Kenya.
- Default currency: KES.
- Kenyan date and number conventions.
- Kenyan-style financial categories.
- Manual M-PESA wallet records.
- Local banks and savings accounts as manually recorded accounts.
- HELB and other personal loans.
- SACCO shares and deposits.
- Kenyan money market funds.
- Treasury bills and government bonds.
- NSE-listed shares and REITs.

It should include categories relevant to local spending, such as rent, food, fare, fuel, airtime, data, water, electricity, SHA, education and household expenses.

Categories should be editable. Not every Kenyan user has the same expenses.

### 10.2 Manual M-PESA tracking

The application should allow a user to create an M-PESA account as a manually maintained financial account.

They should be able to record:

- Money received.
- Payments.
- Withdrawals.
- Deposits.
- Transfers.
- Airtime.
- Transaction fees.
- Other relevant M-PESA activity.

The app must not request SMS-reading permission or use M-PESA API synchronisation in the first release.

The user remains responsible for adding or correcting their entries.

### 10.3 Multi-currency support

The financial foundation should support more than KES.

For example:

- KES.
- USD.
- EUR.
- GBP.
- TZS.
- UGX.
- ZAR.
- Other international currencies.

A user should be able to set a primary currency while holding investments denominated in other currencies.

For example, a Kenyan investor might hold:

- NSE shares in KES.
- US stocks in USD.
- A European ETF in EUR.
- Cryptocurrency denominated in USD.

The app should show:

- Original currency and value.
- Exchange rate used.
- Primary-currency equivalent.
- Exchange-rate date.

If a user manually supplies an exchange rate, this must be distinguishable from a rate fetched from a market data source.

### 10.4 Market data should be introduced carefully

Eventually, the app could retrieve publicly available market prices for supported securities.

That does not require the app to connect to a user's broker or import their transactions.

However, market data involves provider coverage, licensing, reliability and possible costs.

It should therefore be a separate capability with transparent data sources and update times.

For the initial release, manually entered valuations are acceptable. Where a user enters a holding's current value, the app should keep the date and source of that valuation.

### 10.5 Country and financial settings

The app should be designed so users can later configure:

- Country.
- Primary currency.
- Financial year.
- Date format.
- Number format.
- Relevant asset classes.
- Local investment terminology.
- Tax assumptions.

Tax-related calculations should be country-specific and explicitly labelled. The app should not silently apply Kenyan tax rules to someone who lives elsewhere.

## 11. Onboarding and the first-use experience

Onboarding should teach the user how the application works without asking for a large amount of information before they can start.

I would make onboarding a guided process, with the ability to skip optional information and return to it later.

### Step 1: Introduce the product

Explain that the application contains two financial environments:

- Money Management.
- Investment Management.

A short introduction should explain how to switch between them.

Do not bury this interaction in a help page. It is fundamental to the product's identity.

### Step 2: Configure the user's financial settings

Ask for the essential preferences:

- Country.
- Primary currency.
- Preferred theme.
- First environment to open.
- Whether the user wants voice features enabled.

For the initial Kenyan release, KES should be preselected.

### Step 3: Set up Money Management

Guide the user through adding their first account.

Examples:

- Cash.
- Bank.
- M-PESA.
- Savings.

Then ask whether they want to enter their starting balance.

A starting balance should not be recorded as monthly income. It represents money the user already had when beginning to use the app.

### Step 4: Build the first budget

Offer a simple starting structure.

Ask for expected monthly income and a few common spending categories.

The app should help the user create a workable budget without requiring every possible category to be completed.

Users should be able to refine their budgets later.

### Step 5: Set savings and debt details

Ask whether the user wants to add any existing savings goals or outstanding debts.

This should be optional.

The app must not force a user to disclose every debt or financial asset to access the main features.

### Step 6: Set up Investment Management

Let users create their first investment account or holding.

For example, they might enter:

- An NSE share.
- A money market fund.
- A Treasury bill.
- A SACCO deposit.
- A property investment.

The application should explain the difference between entering an asset and recording a new investment purchase.

### Step 7: Introduce privacy and data controls

Explain:

- Where financial information is stored.
- Whether data is synchronised.
- How to export records.
- How to back up and restore data.
- How to use the app lock.
- How voice processing works.
- How to delete the user's information.

Users should understand these choices before entering sensitive financial data.

### Step 8: Enter the correct environment

The user arrives at their selected environment.

The onboarding should end with a usable dashboard, not a long series of promotional screens.

## 12. Privacy, security and data ownership

The privacy-first approach is not just a differentiating selling point. It should influence every major product and architecture decision.

The proposed product principle remains:

> Your money. Your device. Your data.

### 12.1 Local-first storage

I recommend that the financial records be stored locally on the user's phone by default.

The application should be useful without requiring a constant internet connection.

This means:

- Recording transactions should work offline.
- Reviewing budgets should work offline.
- Viewing previously recorded investments should work offline.
- Financial calculations should work offline.
- Editing goals and debts should work offline.
- The user should be able to review historical reports offline.

Actual Budget's local-first design is useful evidence that an offline-oriented personal finance product is a viable design direction.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://actualbudget.org\&sz=32)

Actual Budget+1



The exact data-storage and backup design will need to be chosen by the development team, but it should be designed around user ownership rather than treating a remote account as the only source of truth.

### 12.2 Encryption and app lock

Financial records are sensitive.

The application should provide:

- Protected local financial storage.
- An optional biometric app lock.
- A PIN fallback where appropriate.
- Automatic relocking after a period of inactivity.
- The ability to hide sensitive information from app previews.
- Secure handling of exported backup files.

The user should be able to decide whether the application requires biometric authentication every time it opens or after a selected period.

### 12.3 User-controlled backups

Local storage creates an important responsibility: users need a way to recover their records when they change phones or lose a device.

The app should support:

- Full financial data export.
- Backup creation.
- Restore from backup.
- Clear indication of the backup date.
- A warning before overwriting existing data during restoration.

I recommend an encrypted backup format for sensitive information.

A later version could introduce optional end-to-end encrypted synchronisation between devices, provided users can opt in and the data remains protected.

### 12.4 No selling financial data

The privacy commitment should mean:

- No selling personal financial data.
- No sharing individual financial records with advertisers.
- No advertising profiles derived from spending behaviour.
- No hidden third-party financial data collection.
- No unnecessary access to contacts, SMS or other device information.

The application should also avoid analytics systems that capture actual transaction descriptions, account balances or other confidential records.

### 12.5 Voice privacy

Voice access should not create a loophole in the privacy model.

The user must understand whether the assistant is listening, where voice processing happens and whether any speech is transmitted outside the phone.

The user should be able to use the financial application without ever enabling the microphone.

### 12.6 Authentication and accounts

For a local-first first release, I recommend allowing the user to begin without mandatory registration.

That would make the app easier to start using and reduce the amount of personal information collected.

If accounts or encrypted cloud synchronisation are introduced later, they should be optional and clearly explained.

This is a product recommendation, rather than an already-set requirement. The exact backup and device-transfer experience will influence the final authentication decision.

### 12.7 Offline-first does not mean no internet

Some features may eventually require an internet connection, such as retrieving public market prices or downloading an optional update.

Those features should be isolated from personal financial storage.

The user should be able to see when a feature requires connectivity, and the app should never imply that a stale market price is a live one.

